::: layout notes :::



Tip: Try Plan mode to let the agent perform deep analysis and planning before implementing changes.

did we not find our way more about the layout ecetera? with all these tries

Yes — we learned a bunch about the RX stream/layout, just not in a nice “spec doc” form because Gowin doesn’t hand us one.

What we learned about the Gowin TL RX stream (hard observations)

tl_rx_data[255:0] is effectively 8×DWORD per beat, and tl_rx_valid[7:0] tells you which DWORD lanes are valid in that cycle.
A full TLP can span multiple cycles; tl_rx_sop/tl_rx_eop bracket the packet, but the header DWORD is not guaranteed to be the first collected DWORD. That’s why scanning h=0..9 for the header worked.
The “MWr discriminator” we used is real: the header DWORD has Fmt/Type in bits [31:24] and we repeatedly saw 0x40 / 0x60 show up there (3DW/4DW MWr candidates).
The length field is in header bits [9:0] (we reliably detected LEN==1 for the heartbeat).
Lane ordering matters: collecting lanes in the wrong order within a beat will scramble the word sequence (we explicitly tried reversing the per-beat lane loop).
Address vs payload location relative to the header is consistent once you use the right packing, but the core’s packing made our initial fixed (h+2)/(h+3) assumptions unreliable until we either:
scan for the header, and/or
auto-locate address/payload by behavior (addr stable + offset bits zero, payload toggles 0/1)
What we learned about the actual transaction

TRUEOS is definitely issuing posted Memory Writes to BAR0+0 with a payload that toggles between 0 and 1.
The FPGA is definitely receiving traffic that BAR-decodes to BAR0 and includes MWr TLPs.
End-to-end is now working: decoded payload bit0 reaches the LED output in TRUEGA/min_pci_led/src/top.vhd.
If you want, I can write this up as a short “layout notes” markdown next to the harness (so we don’t lose the map again).